   "text": "This is flurandrenolide (6\u03b1\u2011fluoro\u201111\u03b2,21\u2011dihydroxy\u201116\u03b1,17\u2011isopropylidenedioxypregn\u20114\u2011ene\u20113,20\u2011dione), a steroid acetonide.\n\n**Structure read from the drawing:**\n- Rings A, B, C and D form the usual steroid skeleton. Ring A carries the C3 ketone and the C4=C5 double bond.\n- F is on C6, drawn with a hashed bond.\n- OH is on C11, with a bold wedge.\n- Methyl groups sit on C10 and C13, both drawn as bold wedges.\n- The 16,17\u2011acetonide is a five\u2011membered ring made of C16, C17, two O atoms and a C(CH\u2083)\u2082 carbon.\n- The C17 side chain is C(=O)CH\u2082OH.\n\n**Stereochemistry:**\n- C6, C16 and C17 were assigned from the wedge and hash geometry in the drawing.\n- The centres C8, C9, C10, C11, C13 and C14 were not read from the drawing. The ring\u2011bond wedges at C8\u2013C9 and C14\u2013C15 were ambiguous, so I took them from the SMILES for hydrocortisone (natural steroid configuration) and could not check them against the drawing.\n\n**SMILES check:** every ring closure is opened and closed, and the atom count matches the drawing.\n\nSMILES: C[C@]12CCC(=O)C=C1C[C@@H](F)C[C@@H]3[C@@H]2[C@@H](C[C@]4([C@H]3C[C@H]5OC(C)(C)O[C@@]45C(=O)CO)C)O"
